<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY — a comment ABOVE the root
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent
        xml file" error. Same convention as Lead/validationRules/
        Broker_First_Seen_Is_System_Set and Inbound_Email_Staging__c/fields/Outcome__c.

        GATE A MITIGATION (design-requirements.md §0), FULL TEXT — the <description> below
        restates this in substance but is capped at 1000 characters, so the complete
        rationale lives here:

        PropertyMatchingService.cls (lines 68-72) originally REFUSED to make this
        configurable: "CONFIGURABILITY WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED (design §1.7). This is a
        claim-ARBITRATION constant, so lowering it in Setup retroactively re-decides who
        owns properties that are already claimed — every claim between the new and old
        value releases at once, and the next email on any of them mints a new winner, with
        no deploy, no review and no audit. That is a data migration wearing a checkbox.
        Change it by deploy."

        The user OVERRULED that refusal on 2026-08-30 (GATE A, decision A1: "Make the
        window configurable as asked, and accept the retroactive-release risk"), on the
        condition that the mitigation below ships WITH the configurability, not after it:
          1. THIS field's description restates the warning above, so the risk is visible to
             whoever edits the record in Setup, not only to a reader of Apex.
          2. PropertyMatchingService MUST log the RESOLVED window value at WARN on every
             claim run, so the value in force at the time of a disputed claim is
             reconstructible from logs after the fact. (That half is Apex — the developer/
             technical-architect step that builds BrokerProtectionConfig.cls owns it. This
             field only needs to EXIST and be readable for that logging to be possible.)
        A lowered value here is still an un-audited, retroactive bulk release of live
        broker claims. It is now an accepted operational risk, not an unaddressed one.

        Replaces PropertyMatchingService.PROTECTION_WINDOW_DAYS (currently 90) and is one of
        the five "90 is hardcoded" sites named in that class's own header comment; also
        replaces LeadFunnelController.BP_WINDOW_DAYS (90) and is read by the
        Lead.BP_Expiry__c / Opportunity.BP_Expiry__c display formulas via
        $CustomMetadata.Broker_Protection_Config__mdt.Default.Protection_Window_Days__c —
        see design-requirements.md §2/WS5 for the fallback if that formula shape does not
        validate at API 67.0 (keep the literal 90 in the formulas and assert equality in an
        Apex test instead).
    -->
    <fullName>Protection_Window_Days__c</fullName>
    <description>Days after First Seen a broker&apos;s claim on a property releases, so a later broker can win it (PropertyMatchingService.PROTECTION_WINDOW_DAYS). Also drives the Lead.BP_Expiry__c / Opportunity.BP_Expiry__c display formulas. 🔴 CLAIM-ARBITRATION CONSTANT: lowering this value in Setup retroactively RE-DECIDES who owns properties already claimed — every claim between the old and new value releases AT ONCE, and the next email on any of them mints a new winner, with NO deploy, NO review and NO undo. That is a data migration wearing a checkbox, not a display tweak. MUST stay &lt;= Lookback_Days__c (the accessor clamps this at runtime and logs WARN if violated — do not rely on this field alone). PropertyMatchingService logs the RESOLVED value at WARN on every claim run so the value in force at a disputed claim&apos;s time is reconstructible from logs. See PropertyMatchingService.cls (GATE A, decision A1, 2026-08-30).</description>
    <externalId>false</externalId>
    <fieldManageability>DeveloperControlled</fieldManageability>
    <inlineHelpText>🔴 Claim-arbitration constant. Lowering this retroactively releases live broker claims, all at once, with no undo. See field description before changing.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Protection Window Days</label>
    <precision>3</precision>
    <required>false</required>
    <scale>0</scale>
    <type>Number</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
